                {
                  "phase": "grammar",
                  "type": "grammar_rule",
                  "content": {
                    "tag": "future_forms",
                    "title": "Future forms and might",
                    "explanation": "En inglés hay varias formas de hablar del futuro según lo que queramos expresar:\n- present continuous para planes ya fijados, con hora o lugar: «We're meeting at the studio at five.»\n- «going to» para intenciones: «Are you going to visit us at Christmas?»\n- «will» para promesas, decisiones en el momento y predicciones: «I'll be back, I promise.», «I think they'll like me.»\n- «might» + verbo para posibilidades: «I might go to Cornwall.»\n- present simple para horarios: «The van leaves at twelve.»",
                    "table": {
                      "headers": [
                        "Forma",
                        "Uso",
                        "Ejemplo"
                      ],
                      "rows": [
                        [
                          "Present continuous",
                          "planes fijados (hora, lugar)",
                          "We're meeting at the studio at five."
                        ],
                        [
                          "going to",
                          "intenciones",
                          "Are you going to visit us at Christmas?"
                        ],
                        [
                          "will",
                          "promesas, decisiones, predicciones",
                          "I'll be back for Priya's birthday, I promise."
                        ],
                        [
                          "might",
                          "posibilidad",
                          "I might go to Cornwall."
                        ],
                        [
                          "Present simple",
                          "horarios",
                          "The van leaves at twelve."
                        ]
                      ]
                    },
                    "examples": [
                      "We're starting the recording on Monday.",
                      "I'll be back for Priya's birthday, I promise.",
                      "I might cook them an Irish stew.",
                      "We're flying to Valencia on the fifteenth."
                    ]
                  }
                },
                {
                  "phase": "grammar",
                  "type": "grammar_rule",
                  "content": {
                    "tag": "discourse_markers",
                    "title": "Discourse markers: right, anyway, by the way, actually",
                    "explanation": "En la conversación usamos palabras pequeñas para organizar lo que decimos:\n- «Right», «OK», «So»: para empezar o pasar a otra cosa: «Right, that's the last box.»\n- «Anyway»: para volver al tema o cerrar uno: «Anyway, what are your plans?»\n- «By the way»: para introducir algo que se nos acaba de ocurrir: «By the way, Ollie is moving in early.»\n- «Actually»: en realidad, para corregir o añadir algo inesperado (no significa actualmente): «I'm not nervous. Well, actually, I am a bit.»\n- «Well»: para pensar o suavizar una respuesta: «Well, maybe.»\n- «I mean»: para aclarar: «He's nervous. I mean, he's meeting my parents!»\n\nOjo con el falso amigo: actualmente = «currently» / «at the moment».",
                    "table": {
                      "headers": [
                        "Marcador",
                        "Para…",
                        "Ejemplo"
                      ],
                      "rows": [
                        [
                          "right / so",
                          "empezar, pasar a otra cosa",
                          "Right, let's carry the boxes."
                        ],
                        [
                          "anyway",
                          "volver al tema",
                          "Anyway, when are you leaving?"
                        ],
                        [
                          "by the way",
                          "añadir algo de pasada",
                          "By the way, I've got your keys."
                        ],
                        [
                          "actually",
                          "corregir, sorprender",
                          "Actually, I'm moving in today."
                        ],
                        [
                          "I mean",
                          "aclarar",
                          "It's hot. I mean, really hot."
                        ]
                      ]
                    },
                    "examples": [
                      "Right, that's the last box. Let's go.",
                      "Anyway, what are your plans for the summer?",
                      "By the way, Ollie is moving in today.",
                      "I'm not nervous. Well, actually, I'm a bit nervous."
                    ]
                  }
                },
